00:37:09,535 --> 00:37:11,636
[SPEAKER_00]: In the sense of I'm being subjected to it.

604
00:37:11,656 --> 00:37:11,936
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

605
00:37:13,616 --> 00:37:15,677
[SPEAKER_00]: But if I see that image is just there,

606
00:37:17,974 --> 00:37:22,216
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to see that image is just there, and it's... but there's an image for me.

607
00:37:22,777 --> 00:37:22,997
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

608
00:37:24,717 --> 00:37:25,418
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's an image.

609
00:37:25,678 --> 00:37:25,878
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

610
00:37:26,458 --> 00:37:28,359
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's secondary to image.

611
00:37:29,360 --> 00:37:29,680
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not.

612
00:37:29,740 --> 00:37:30,520
[SPEAKER_02]: Why is it secondary?

613
00:37:31,381 --> 00:37:31,881
[SPEAKER_02]: It's an image.

614
00:37:33,062 --> 00:37:35,803
[SPEAKER_02]: Secondary only in your assumed relationship between the two images.

615
00:37:36,323 --> 00:37:37,204
[SPEAKER_02]: The content of it.

616
00:37:37,864 --> 00:37:39,185
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the content of for me?

617
00:37:42,306 --> 00:37:45,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's different than content of like a... like this is not

618
00:37:50,384 --> 00:37:52,686
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the content of that tape?

619
00:37:52,986 --> 00:37:58,812
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the content of it?

620
00:38:00,073 --> 00:38:01,734
[SPEAKER_02]: And does color have a content?

621
00:38:02,855 --> 00:38:08,240
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I'm saying that when you say content...

622
00:38:08,300 --> 00:38:14,165
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's another image that you yet somehow assume to be the material content of my image.

623
00:38:15,928 --> 00:38:16,968
[SPEAKER_02]: As if that's not an image.

624
00:38:17,328 --> 00:38:24,270
[SPEAKER_02]: As if that's not an image, which means any sense of materiality or mentality has to be within an image.

625
00:38:24,290 --> 00:38:25,351
[SPEAKER_02]: So, type of image.

626
00:38:27,051 --> 00:38:27,831
[SPEAKER_02]: Type of image.

627
00:38:28,372 --> 00:38:29,472
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, why would you worry about that?

628
00:38:30,352 --> 00:38:33,393
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just, instead of two images, start thinking about one.

629
00:38:34,640 --> 00:38:36,701
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you said, this is for me.

630
00:38:37,521 --> 00:38:39,462
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's an image of this, there's an image for me.

631
00:38:39,522 --> 00:38:40,602
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is more important there?

632
00:38:40,902 --> 00:38:42,203
[SPEAKER_02]: The image of this or the image for me?

633
00:38:42,543 --> 00:38:45,604
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, the image for me, because it's an account for me that I feel and suffer.

634
00:38:46,444 --> 00:38:50,845
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have an image for me, which has a nature of an image.

635
00:38:51,145 --> 00:38:58,568
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you understand the nature of an image for me, you understood the nature of all images that are for you, which is literally anything you will be perceiving.

636
00:39:00,428 --> 00:39:00,928
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean.

637
00:39:00,948 --> 00:39:04,051
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like comparing and figuring out the relation between the two images.

638
00:39:04,091 --> 00:39:04,311
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

639
00:39:04,331 --> 00:39:07,554
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're failing to understand this common nature of it, which is, it's an image.

640
00:39:08,214 --> 00:39:08,595
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the...

641
00:39:09,355 --> 00:39:16,041
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you're thinking about an image that doesn't pertain for me, means now you're thinking like a second-hand, sort of, abstractly.

642
00:39:17,282 --> 00:39:18,763
[SPEAKER_02]: You want to make it relevant.

643
00:39:18,783 --> 00:39:25,288
[SPEAKER_02]: Relevant on... which means on the fundamental level of the experience, which is where the appropriation is, where the sense of ownership, sense of atta, everything is.

644
00:39:26,129 --> 00:39:28,731
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like a suffering.

645
00:39:30,525 --> 00:39:30,905
[SPEAKER_02]: Suffering.

646
00:39:30,965 --> 00:39:31,326
[SPEAKER_02]: Suffering.

647
00:39:31,446 --> 00:39:32,046
[SPEAKER_02]: What is suffering?

648
00:39:33,968 --> 00:39:35,189
[SPEAKER_00]: You're telling me it's an image.

649
00:39:35,249 --> 00:39:35,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever.

650
00:39:35,609 --> 00:39:36,169
[SPEAKER_02]: What is suffering?

651
00:39:36,189 --> 00:39:37,851
[SPEAKER_02]: When I say suffering, what's present in your mind?

652
00:39:37,911 --> 00:39:38,251
[SPEAKER_02]: Suffering.

653
00:39:38,471 --> 00:39:40,252
[SPEAKER_02]: What is suffering?

654
00:39:40,272 --> 00:39:45,977
[SPEAKER_00]: The pain and the discomfort.

655
00:39:46,337 --> 00:39:47,598
[SPEAKER_02]: The pain and the discomfort.

656
00:39:49,039 --> 00:39:51,741
[SPEAKER_02]: Stands for the image of suffering.

657
00:39:51,781 --> 00:39:53,322
[SPEAKER_02]: It's an image of suffering.

658
00:39:53,362 --> 00:39:54,043
[SPEAKER_02]: Image of pain.

659
00:39:55,504 --> 00:39:56,705
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is the image of pain then?

660
00:39:56,885 --> 00:39:58,626
[SPEAKER_02]: What are the characteristics of the image of pain?

661
00:39:58,766 --> 00:39:59,527
[SPEAKER_02]: How would you describe it?

662
00:40:01,127 --> 00:40:03,548
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is the necessary image for the image of suffering.

663
00:40:07,270 --> 00:40:08,050
[SPEAKER_02]: What's the image of pain?

664
00:40:09,251 --> 00:40:14,734
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's not... What is the image of pain?

665
00:40:16,074 --> 00:40:16,695
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, again...

666
00:40:16,995 --> 00:40:23,578
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't want to... Whatever you say, it's probably going to be more accurate than to try to think about what it is.

667
00:40:25,340 --> 00:40:28,681
[SPEAKER_02]: Because when I say the image of pain, it's not like, where is the image of pain?

668
00:40:29,301 --> 00:40:34,103
[SPEAKER_02]: The fact that you're thinking about something, means there is the image of that there, to that extent.

669
00:40:35,143 --> 00:40:39,584
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm asking you, tell me the extent of the image of pain, when I ask you, what is the image of pain?

670
00:40:40,125 --> 00:40:41,825
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't tell you, tell me the right image of pain.

671
00:40:42,285 --> 00:40:43,466
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the extent of the pain?

672
00:40:44,646 --> 00:40:45,186
[SPEAKER_02]: When you hear...

673
00:40:45,386 --> 00:40:49,368
[SPEAKER_00]: The displeasure, the uncomfortable, the not wanted.

674
00:40:49,768 --> 00:40:50,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Not wanted.

675
00:40:52,616 --> 00:41:00,422
[SPEAKER_02]: So, all these things have in common no, no, no, no.

676
00:41:00,562 --> 00:41:02,343
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a common attitude to all these things.

677
00:41:04,065 --> 00:41:08,708
[SPEAKER_02]: Displeasure, discomfort, no wanting is no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no.

678
00:41:09,669 --> 00:41:10,810
[SPEAKER_02]: Is no in things?

679
00:41:12,231 --> 00:41:14,212
[SPEAKER_02]: Or is no in your attitude in regard to things?

680
00:41:16,794 --> 00:41:17,435
[SPEAKER_00]: In your attitude.

681
00:41:18,255 --> 00:41:20,998
[SPEAKER_02]: In your attitude in regard to things you are subjected to.

682
00:41:22,032 --> 00:41:26,874
[SPEAKER_02]: So, where is then the suffering that you said that has a necessary image?

683
00:41:27,554 --> 00:41:29,575
[SPEAKER_00]: The resisting of it.

684
00:41:29,915 --> 00:41:32,416
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's basically that the suffering is not an image of pain.

685
00:41:33,437 --> 00:41:36,238
[SPEAKER_02]: The suffering is in you saying no to the image of pain.

686
00:41:38,018 --> 00:41:42,340
[SPEAKER_02]: But how can you even say no to an image of pain, to an image, without an image?

687
00:41:45,361 --> 00:41:48,823
[SPEAKER_02]: Because saying no to the image of pain implies you having

688
00:41:49,718 --> 00:41:57,464
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, saying no to the reasoned image implies you having... like, you can move outside of the space of... that has a reason based on the base of that image.

689
00:41:58,245 --> 00:42:02,568
[SPEAKER_02]: Saying no to this means you can replace it with something else from the outside.

690
00:42:03,369 --> 00:42:05,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, all that is within this you're saying no to.

691
00:42:05,891 --> 00:42:11,315
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're responding to an image that you don't want with another image.

692
00:42:11,335 --> 00:42:11,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

693
00:42:15,419 --> 00:42:18,121
[SPEAKER_02]: So, discerning the nature of images will free you from suffering, then.

694
00:42:19,432 --> 00:42:21,092
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the image of pain is not the suffering.

695
00:42:22,613 --> 00:42:24,273
[SPEAKER_02]: The image of discomfort is not the suffering.

696
00:42:25,493 --> 00:42:30,034
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when I say I'm afflicted by images, that's an image?

697
00:42:30,734 --> 00:42:31,294
[SPEAKER_00]: That's an image.

698
00:42:31,894 --> 00:42:32,994
[SPEAKER_00]: Which I'm afflicted by?

699
00:42:33,655 --> 00:42:33,895
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

700
00:42:33,915 --> 00:42:34,455
[SPEAKER_00]: That's an image.

701
00:42:35,115 --> 00:42:36,315
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of another image?

702
00:42:36,915 --> 00:42:37,535
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is not...

703
00:42:37,675 --> 00:42:41,336
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is your attitude of this, which you haven't been seeing as an image.

704
00:42:41,356 --> 00:42:42,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I'm separating that.

705
00:42:42,836 --> 00:42:47,037
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what the necessary reason for the right view is seeing the sign of your mind.

706
00:42:48,112 --> 00:42:53,937
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, within the now established sign of where your mind is, you can start to discern these images.

707
00:42:53,998 --> 00:42:54,898
[SPEAKER_00]: Sign of the mind is?

708
00:42:55,359 --> 00:42:55,899
[SPEAKER_02]: An image.

709
00:42:56,620 --> 00:43:00,323
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the most general image of where my mind is.

710
00:43:00,343 --> 00:43:04,267
[SPEAKER_02]: Which most people engage with the senses in the world on day-to-day level, they don't see it.

711
00:43:04,347 --> 00:43:07,310
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's a necessary condition for becoming Sotāpanna.

712
00:43:08,711 --> 00:43:09,432
[SPEAKER_02]: Sign of the mind.

713
00:43:10,836 --> 00:43:12,697
[SPEAKER_02]: But now, this is even further what we're speaking about.

714
00:43:12,737 --> 00:43:19,500
[SPEAKER_02]: It's basically seeing the presence of the enduring, resisting images within that mind of me or whatever else.

715
00:43:21,061 --> 00:43:28,465
[SPEAKER_02]: So, not seeing, exactly, not seeing your attitude of... not seeing, basically, the attitude of resisting as an image.

716
00:43:29,898 --> 00:43:34,203
[SPEAKER_02]: You kind of give it gratuitously, give it more validity on account of all the images.

717
00:43:34,263 --> 00:43:37,606
[SPEAKER_02]: You assume it separate from all the images you're resisting to.

718
00:43:37,966 --> 00:43:42,410
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you be resisting an image if there is no image there to begin with?

719
00:43:43,532 --> 00:43:47,896
[SPEAKER_02]: If there is no image of pain, can you be resisting it?

720
00:43:50,618 --> 00:43:51,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

721
00:43:52,459 --> 00:44:00,464
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, you somehow assume that you resisting it is the first, and then just these things come, and are moving them away, and chasing them away, and accepting them, and rejecting them.

722
00:44:01,785 --> 00:44:06,988
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it's kind of... it's kind of like phenomenology, just recognizing...

723
00:44:07,028 --> 00:44:08,069
[SPEAKER_01]: In the true sense, yeah.

724
00:44:08,089 --> 00:44:18,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, taken to the... to the extreme of basically, you know, just recognizing that there are these images there, these phenomena there, that normally... Have to be there.

725
00:44:18,696 --> 00:44:21,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and that... that... yeah, that can't not be there.

726
00:44:22,712 --> 00:44:27,135
[SPEAKER_01]: And they're so close to you, that you normally look through them and take them for granted.

727
00:44:27,355 --> 00:44:27,556
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

728
00:44:27,796 --> 00:44:32,920
[SPEAKER_02]: And you will be taken for granted, because by looking through them, you start seeing further through.

729
00:44:33,120 --> 00:44:33,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

730
00:44:34,140 --> 00:44:37,863
[SPEAKER_02]: And that you see further through, and that's how the whole sensuality proliferates as well.

731
00:44:38,604 --> 00:44:40,945
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're also not indifferent to these things.

732
00:44:41,606 --> 00:44:45,248
[SPEAKER_02]: Every image is simultaneously felt pleasantly or pleasantly or neutrally.

733
00:44:45,669 --> 00:44:46,109
[SPEAKER_02]: Has to be.

734
00:44:47,270 --> 00:44:48,391
[SPEAKER_02]: One of those things has to be.

735
00:44:49,207 --> 00:44:58,394
[SPEAKER_02]: And given that you have attitude in wanting pleasure, avoiding pain, means some images will be wanted, which means you'll be going for the content, failing to see the nature of the image.

736
00:44:58,994 --> 00:45:03,517
[SPEAKER_02]: And another images will be rejected, means trying to deny the content, failing to see the nature of the image.

737
00:45:04,458 --> 00:45:05,759
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you remain bound with the image.

738
00:45:08,269 --> 00:45:16,275
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to see that which is wanted, that which is not wanted, as images, as that sort of common ground... That's what I mean, like, even these ambiguous things.

739
00:45:16,315 --> 00:45:16,815
[SPEAKER_02]: Wanting.

740
00:45:17,336 --> 00:45:18,737
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you understand what wanting is?

741
00:45:19,197 --> 00:45:19,978
[SPEAKER_02]: Wanting, wanting.

742
00:45:20,058 --> 00:45:22,620
[SPEAKER_02]: How would you describe wanting?

743
00:45:22,640 --> 00:45:23,640
[SPEAKER_02]: Take a particular example.

744
00:45:24,121 --> 00:45:24,601
[SPEAKER_02]: I want it.

745
00:45:24,681 --> 00:45:25,402
[SPEAKER_02]: I want to eat.

746
00:45:26,528 --> 00:45:30,169
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is... what is my wanting in this particular instance?

747
00:45:30,189 --> 00:45:31,610
[SPEAKER_02]: It implies... Yeah.

748
00:45:32,150 --> 00:45:32,470
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

749
00:45:32,550 --> 00:45:32,930
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

750
00:45:32,970 --> 00:45:34,231
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it has to be me.

751
00:45:34,251 --> 00:45:35,551
[SPEAKER_02]: It has to be that thing.

752
00:45:35,631 --> 00:45:35,811
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

753
00:45:36,491 --> 00:45:38,612
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can do both.

754
00:45:38,652 --> 00:45:39,713
[SPEAKER_02]: First, take that thing.

755
00:45:40,373 --> 00:45:43,014
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you have any say in the manifestation of an image of that thing?

756
00:45:43,814 --> 00:45:49,116
[SPEAKER_02]: If that thing is not already... Because, you see, I'm not seeing the food now, yet I know I want food.

757
00:45:49,616 --> 00:45:50,296
[SPEAKER_02]: How is that possible?

758
00:45:51,157 --> 00:45:54,978
[SPEAKER_02]: If the food is genuinely, entirely, solely, fully material,

759
00:45:55,857 --> 00:45:56,918
[SPEAKER_02]: How can I even think it?

760
00:45:57,938 --> 00:45:58,259
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not.

761
00:45:58,759 --> 00:46:04,182
[SPEAKER_02]: Material and mental are images first, and they always remain images.

762
00:46:04,202 --> 00:46:10,867
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, they're images that are sort of images of the senses, and they're images that are images of the mind.

763
00:46:11,927 --> 00:46:12,688
[SPEAKER_02]: But they're all images.

764
00:46:15,630 --> 00:46:20,793
[SPEAKER_02]: And while the senses, the five senses operate, they will be the images of the senses.

765
00:46:20,813 --> 00:46:23,575
[SPEAKER_02]: While the mind is there, the mind operates, they will also be the image of the mind.

766
00:46:27,875 --> 00:46:34,342
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not that the images of the senses and then there are also, you know, the real objects of those senses.

767
00:46:34,602 --> 00:46:36,164
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, object of the sense.

768
00:46:36,904 --> 00:46:38,466
[SPEAKER_02]: Image upon image upon image upon image.

769
00:46:39,928 --> 00:46:40,848
[SPEAKER_02]: Or an image.

770
00:46:40,869 --> 00:46:43,031
[SPEAKER_02]: So, seeing image.

771
00:46:44,072 --> 00:46:45,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Means seeing the mind coming first.

772
00:46:45,794 --> 00:46:48,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing image or an always seeing image.

773
00:46:50,430 --> 00:46:51,311
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you don't need to...

774
00:46:51,771 --> 00:46:53,172
[SPEAKER_02]: It's really discerning an image.

775
00:46:53,292 --> 00:46:57,655
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like I always have to be seeing the image, because you have no choice but to see anything else.

776
00:46:57,675 --> 00:46:58,475
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, discerning that.

777
00:46:58,935 --> 00:47:00,036
[SPEAKER_02]: But discerning what you're seeing.

778
00:47:00,056 --> 00:47:04,719
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you... Putujana sees images, that's all he can see.

779
00:47:04,879 --> 00:47:06,560
[SPEAKER_02]: Arahant sees images, that's all he can see.

780
00:47:06,660 --> 00:47:10,623
[SPEAKER_02]: The difference is, for Putujana, he misconceives images to be there, which they're not.

781
00:47:11,730 --> 00:47:13,371
[SPEAKER_01]: ...as being separate from his mind.

782
00:47:13,651 --> 00:47:13,831
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

783
00:47:14,091 --> 00:47:16,612
[SPEAKER_02]: Whereas... Well, then the whole idea of separation comes from it.

784
00:47:16,652 --> 00:47:20,093
[SPEAKER_02]: Assuming external, internal, both, neither mine, not mine.

785
00:47:20,413 --> 00:47:24,675
[SPEAKER_01]: And what we're talking about here is the direction of unification of mind, of samādhi.

786
00:47:24,975 --> 00:47:26,115
[SPEAKER_02]: Unification, exactly.

787
00:47:26,375 --> 00:47:26,535
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

788
00:47:26,795 --> 00:47:27,616
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

789
00:47:27,636 --> 00:47:27,976
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

790
00:47:28,036 --> 00:47:31,357
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you discern the unifying context, the unifying nature.

791
00:47:32,077 --> 00:47:38,780
[SPEAKER_02]: That's when I ask you first, I say, what's in common to you looking at the tape measure and you thinking about not seeing it?

792
00:47:38,880 --> 00:47:39,620
[SPEAKER_02]: What's in common?

793
00:47:40,397 --> 00:47:49,821
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's training in regard to the ambiguity of images, not to clarify it into detail, but to recognize it, the extent of its ambiguity.

794
00:47:50,461 --> 00:47:59,484
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's only ambiguity of the content, ambiguity of the detail, not the ambiguity of the nature of images that I'm being subjected to.

795
00:47:59,504 --> 00:47:59,944
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean.

796
00:48:00,585 --> 00:48:08,868
[SPEAKER_02]: And not being able to grip with that kind of recognition of that ambiguity, that's why you don't, like, wanting, not wanting, all these terms become abstract for most people.

797
00:48:11,355 --> 00:48:17,257
[SPEAKER_02]: And later on, if you start thinking about emptiness, not-self, impermanence, well, imagine how abstract that is.

798
00:48:18,817 --> 00:48:20,318
[SPEAKER_02]: An abstract means you're not seeing it.

799
00:48:20,458 --> 00:48:20,818
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

800
00:48:22,219 --> 00:48:23,319
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not seeing an image of it.

801
00:48:24,079 --> 00:48:27,700
[SPEAKER_02]: You're thinking it, which is only the second one.

802
00:48:27,720 --> 00:48:30,941
[SPEAKER_01]: You have a thought, but you aren't seeing that thought as the image.

803
00:48:30,961 --> 00:48:31,542
[SPEAKER_01]: As an image.

804
00:48:31,582 --> 00:48:32,382
[SPEAKER_01]: As the image that it is.

805
00:48:32,882 --> 00:48:33,182
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

806
00:48:33,442 --> 00:48:35,123
[SPEAKER_02]: As an image, and you're misconceiving that thought.

807
00:48:35,143 --> 00:48:35,463
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

808
00:48:36,861 --> 00:48:38,542
[SPEAKER_02]: Because there is an image there, whether you see it or not.

809
00:48:39,082 --> 00:48:39,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

810
00:48:39,522 --> 00:48:43,803
[SPEAKER_02]: So, literally, any attitude is a wanting.

811
00:48:44,643 --> 00:48:44,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

812
00:48:45,264 --> 00:48:46,464
[SPEAKER_02]: So, wanting requires you.

813
00:48:47,504 --> 00:48:50,045
[SPEAKER_02]: So, let's dwell on the you aspect of wanting.

814
00:48:50,685 --> 00:48:50,905
[SPEAKER_02]: Me.

815
00:48:51,465 --> 00:48:52,366
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the image of me?

816
00:48:52,506 --> 00:48:53,586
[SPEAKER_02]: First image that comes to mind.

817
00:48:53,626 --> 00:48:55,647
[SPEAKER_02]: The most ambiguous, distant, me.

818
00:48:56,147 --> 00:48:56,907
[SPEAKER_02]: Me, me, me.

819
00:48:57,247 --> 00:48:57,987
[SPEAKER_02]: Shape of a body.

820
00:48:59,148 --> 00:48:59,508
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

821
00:49:00,248 --> 00:49:01,408
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's what it stands for.

822
00:49:01,428 --> 00:49:01,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

823
00:49:02,369 --> 00:49:06,230
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not thinking about it, then you end up having all this attitude that it's more than that.

824
00:49:07,644 --> 00:49:10,025
[SPEAKER_02]: Whenever you think, that's all it is.

825
00:49:10,065 --> 00:49:18,048
[SPEAKER_02]: Shape of the body, memory of the face that you see in reflection, first point of view, yeah, place, me, this.

826
00:49:19,008 --> 00:49:29,332
[SPEAKER_02]: So, while you have that image of shape of the body as me, which is me, do you have any say in how that shape of the body has been manifested for me?

827
00:49:29,412 --> 00:49:31,013
[SPEAKER_02]: Subjective, what came before, after?

828
00:49:31,073 --> 00:49:34,094
[SPEAKER_02]: Can I even uproot it somehow, step outside of it?

829
00:49:34,714 --> 00:49:36,975
[SPEAKER_02]: Or am I always secondary to that shape of the body?

830
00:49:38,835 --> 00:49:43,857
[SPEAKER_02]: Or that memory of the face, of my own face, that stands for, when I think, me, my own name.

831
00:49:45,318 --> 00:49:45,478
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

832
00:49:46,138 --> 00:49:49,899
[SPEAKER_02]: That's... you... you are basically locked outside of it.

833
00:49:50,519 --> 00:49:52,980
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, sorry, locked inside of it, more accurately.

834
00:49:53,000 --> 00:49:56,642
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if you look left, right, up or down, whatever, you're within it.

835
00:49:58,430 --> 00:50:04,214
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, so, can I, can I, how can I then be the owner of this, when all I'm doing is being subjected to these images?

836
00:50:04,474 --> 00:50:06,315
[SPEAKER_02]: Images of me... That's all unsubjective as well.

837
00:50:06,495 --> 00:50:07,776
[SPEAKER_02]: That's all unsubjective.

838
00:50:07,796 --> 00:50:08,917
[SPEAKER_02]: There's nothing outside that.

839
00:50:09,217 --> 00:50:10,058
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, but there's more.

840
00:50:10,298 --> 00:50:12,580
[SPEAKER_02]: There's an image of more.

841
00:50:12,640 --> 00:50:16,062
[SPEAKER_02]: An image of ambiguity, a biguity of an extent.

842
00:50:16,362 --> 00:50:16,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

843
00:50:16,842 --> 00:50:17,203
[SPEAKER_02]: More.

844
00:50:18,063 --> 00:50:18,984
[SPEAKER_02]: Ah, but that's an image.

845
00:50:19,104 --> 00:50:19,284
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

846
00:50:19,644 --> 00:50:20,385
[SPEAKER_02]: That I'm subjected to.

847
00:50:20,425 --> 00:50:21,386
[SPEAKER_02]: That I'm subjected to.

848
00:50:23,267 --> 00:50:27,770
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh, that's, I don't like the, you know,

849
00:50:28,719 --> 00:50:31,220
[SPEAKER_00]: I am subjected to images.

850
00:50:31,240 --> 00:50:31,460
[SPEAKER_02]: Hmm.

851
00:50:32,140 --> 00:50:37,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying that, because it gives me the impression that I... Well, it gives you space to be separate from it.

852
00:50:37,942 --> 00:50:39,123
[SPEAKER_00]: I am subjected to these images.

853
00:50:39,143 --> 00:50:40,423
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to start like that.

854
00:50:40,603 --> 00:50:41,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure, sure.

855
00:50:41,963 --> 00:50:43,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Could you... What about the body?

856
00:50:43,944 --> 00:50:44,564
[SPEAKER_01]: The senses?

857
00:50:44,984 --> 00:50:45,225
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

858
00:50:45,705 --> 00:50:46,165
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

859
00:50:46,185 --> 00:50:48,005
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is subjected to images.

860
00:50:49,106 --> 00:50:50,546
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the nature... that's what a mind is.

861
00:50:50,726 --> 00:50:52,727
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't... there can't be mind without images.

862
00:50:54,460 --> 00:51:01,665
[SPEAKER_00]: But if I say manifestation is subjected to... Yeah, that's absolutely fine.

863
00:51:01,785 --> 00:51:06,068
[SPEAKER_02]: If that becomes a more accurate way of describing it for you, that's fine.

864
00:51:06,128 --> 00:51:11,372
[SPEAKER_02]: But for most people, in order to start, you have to start like that and then, eventually, come to click.

865
00:51:12,052 --> 00:51:15,875
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, but I'm subjected to this, I'm subjected to that, I'm subjected to that.

866
00:51:16,576 --> 00:51:16,856
[SPEAKER_02]: I am.

867
00:51:17,556 --> 00:51:17,776
[SPEAKER_02]: I am.

868
00:51:18,117 --> 00:51:18,297
[SPEAKER_02]: Hmm.

869
00:51:18,917 --> 00:51:21,139
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, isn't that another image I'm subjected to?

870
00:51:21,759 --> 00:51:24,041
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you get to upgrade your own description.

871
00:51:25,223 --> 00:51:35,851
[SPEAKER_02]: But, as I said, the starting point is the point of ignorance, the point of misconceiving, which means you will not be able to see immediately things like the Arahant does.

872
00:51:35,891 --> 00:51:39,394
[SPEAKER_02]: Approximating how Arahant sees, you get to understand what Arahant is.

873
00:51:40,695 --> 00:51:41,855
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is what makes you become one.

874
00:51:42,056 --> 00:51:45,178
[SPEAKER_00]: All dhammas are another, not self.

875
00:51:46,779 --> 00:51:49,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Because not self is an image.

876
00:51:49,361 --> 00:51:49,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

877
00:51:50,142 --> 00:51:51,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Or self is an image.

878
00:51:52,395 --> 00:52:06,442
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you could say that, yeah, the sense of self, it's, as you say, as we kind of just described, it's kind of a... becomes a more fundamental image than everything else that comes through your senses.

879
00:52:06,462 --> 00:52:09,943
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, whether it's seeing, hearing, tasting, touching, as you said yourself, it's for me.

880
00:52:10,464 --> 00:52:13,045
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's... that image is in common to all these senses.

881
00:52:14,383 --> 00:52:16,764
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what if I were to then understand and abandon that image?

882
00:52:16,825 --> 00:52:20,227
[SPEAKER_02]: Abandon in the sense of experience dispassion and remove the ownership of it?

883
00:52:20,787 --> 00:52:23,128
[SPEAKER_02]: You have removed the ownership of the five senses.

884
00:52:23,629 --> 00:52:28,031
[SPEAKER_00]: You have removed sensuality, sense desires.

885
00:52:28,131 --> 00:52:31,974
[SPEAKER_00]: By removing the ownership of this image of self.

886
00:52:32,254 --> 00:52:32,454
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

887
00:52:32,954 --> 00:52:33,635
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

888
00:52:33,675 --> 00:52:35,196
[SPEAKER_02]: That all these things come through.

889
00:52:35,276 --> 00:52:43,321
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what we meant by establishing your mind upon the totality of the extent of solidity, totality of the extent of

890
00:52:47,185 --> 00:52:52,666
[SPEAKER_02]: Basically, by practicing kasiṇas, you establish your mind to a certain level, everything inferior to that is abandoned.

891
00:52:53,026 --> 00:52:55,907
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, you know, perceptions of the forest abandon perceptions of the village.

892
00:52:56,427 --> 00:52:58,408
[SPEAKER_02]: They had inferior perceptions in regard to it.

893
00:52:58,788 --> 00:53:05,990
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, either way, which doesn't matter how particular perceptions get, this is what they have in common, and you abandon that common ground.

894
00:53:06,770 --> 00:53:10,291
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whether it's sight, sound, smell, taste, touch, the common ground is image of me.

895
00:53:10,831 --> 00:53:11,191
[SPEAKER_02]: For me.

896
00:53:11,792 --> 00:53:12,132
[SPEAKER_02]: Mine.

897
00:53:12,992 --> 00:53:13,292
[SPEAKER_02]: I am.

898
00:53:14,438 --> 00:53:21,742
[SPEAKER_02]: You abandon that, you abandon all these inferior images within that image.

899
00:53:21,762 --> 00:53:26,505
[SPEAKER_02]: And by abandoning means not preventing it from arising, because you haven't created its arising to begin with.

900
00:53:26,685 --> 00:53:27,646
[SPEAKER_02]: It was never a problem.

901
00:53:28,647 --> 00:53:33,550
[SPEAKER_02]: Abandoning it means removing the gratuitous attitudes of resisting it or craving for it.

902
00:53:36,111 --> 00:53:38,833
[SPEAKER_02]: And you will crave for things for as long as you think you can crave for things.

903
00:53:40,074 --> 00:53:44,836
[SPEAKER_02]: Full understanding that craving is impossible, the way you assumed it, that's how you stop assuming.

904
00:53:44,876 --> 00:53:46,897
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you crave for something that's already there?

905
00:53:47,718 --> 00:53:48,098
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

906
00:53:52,080 --> 00:53:56,181
[SPEAKER_02]: As I said, oh, I don't see the food now, but I want to eat.

907
00:53:56,442 --> 00:53:57,402
[SPEAKER_02]: I crave the food.

908
00:53:57,422 --> 00:53:58,743
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's say I crave the food.

909
00:54:00,363 --> 00:54:06,066
[SPEAKER_02]: If my craving is in the food, if the food is the craving, I will need to be in contact with it, in order to crave for it.

910
00:54:06,615 --> 00:54:07,876
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, I don't even have food.

911
00:54:08,076 --> 00:54:09,297
[SPEAKER_02]: But you are in contact with it.

912
00:54:09,717 --> 00:54:10,058
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

913
00:54:10,238 --> 00:54:12,479
[SPEAKER_02]: But not the content of the domain that I'm craving for.

914
00:54:12,519 --> 00:54:13,020
[SPEAKER_01]: With the image.

915
00:54:13,380 --> 00:54:13,720
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

916
00:54:14,100 --> 00:54:19,984
[SPEAKER_02]: So you realize, oh, so my craving is then on the level of the image, yet, I don't stay on the level of the image when I crave.

917
00:54:20,265 --> 00:54:21,666
[SPEAKER_02]: I go out and seek food.

918
00:54:21,786 --> 00:54:22,226
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

919
00:54:22,306 --> 00:54:25,328
[SPEAKER_02]: And steal food, and attack, and kill, and work, and earn, whatever.

920
00:54:25,848 --> 00:54:32,293
[SPEAKER_02]: Rightly or wrongly, I go out and seek these objects, because I crave in regard to the image of food.

921
00:54:35,627 --> 00:54:40,391
[SPEAKER_02]: And that gets paired then with a discomfort of hunger.

922
00:54:40,411 --> 00:54:42,873
[SPEAKER_02]: So, they're naturally associating then.

923
00:54:43,013 --> 00:54:53,843
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeking this food that I can put in my belly alleviates, helps my gratuitous attitude of resisting pain of physical nature, discomfort of hunger.

924
00:54:55,164 --> 00:54:58,306
[SPEAKER_02]: But discomfort of hunger, again, it's an image.

925
00:55:00,268 --> 00:55:03,611
[SPEAKER_02]: An image that literally you can put anything in your mouth, eat it.

926
00:55:04,671 --> 00:55:09,393
[SPEAKER_02]: You wouldn't worry about the taste then, because you fully stay with the discomfort of the hunger as the context.

927
00:55:09,453 --> 00:55:12,575
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, I have to deal with this discomfort, because the body will die.

928
00:55:13,055 --> 00:55:14,635
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't need to crave for it in order to do it.

929
00:55:16,136 --> 00:55:17,537
[SPEAKER_02]: But most people don't act on that level.

930
00:55:17,557 --> 00:55:23,599
[SPEAKER_02]: Most people act on another whole layer added to it, which is, yeah, I'm hungry, but I want particular taste.

931
00:55:26,420 --> 00:55:29,702
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's another image that can never be anything other than an image.

932
00:55:30,909 --> 00:55:36,873
[SPEAKER_02]: But you assume taste to be in the objects of your senses, which is another image.

933
00:55:38,714 --> 00:55:42,477
[SPEAKER_02]: But you assume the materiality of it, independent of you.

934
00:55:42,917 --> 00:55:44,418
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why sensuality is wrong.

935
00:55:45,579 --> 00:55:49,762
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's a perversion of the right order of things.

936
00:55:50,842 --> 00:55:53,704
[SPEAKER_02]: If you weren't perverting the order, it wouldn't be unwholesome.

937
00:55:55,365 --> 00:55:56,006
[SPEAKER_02]: It wouldn't be wrong.

938
00:55:57,150 --> 00:55:58,951
[SPEAKER_02]: So, things that don't pervert the order are not wrong.

939
00:55:59,632 --> 00:56:03,234
[SPEAKER_02]: Such as hunger, putting something in your stomach so you're not hungry.

940
00:56:05,515 --> 00:56:09,818
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't... you don't... you don't misconceive what comes first.

941
00:56:09,838 --> 00:56:13,560
[SPEAKER_02]: And the purpose then cannot become corrupted.

942
00:56:14,379 --> 00:56:23,042
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the whole instruction of the Buddha, to mindfully... eating mindfully, as in not observe every movement of your tongue, but mindful of the purpose why you're eating.

943
00:56:23,642 --> 00:56:26,143
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, I will eat for this reason, for this reason, for...

944
00:56:26,203 --> 00:56:36,066
[SPEAKER_02]: I will not eat for this reason, for... That's the mindfulness of eating, not chewing, chewing, slowly, slowly... Choking, choking, choking... Yeah.

945
00:56:36,853 --> 00:56:36,973
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

946
00:56:37,293 --> 00:56:38,094
[SPEAKER_02]: Why am I eating?

947
00:56:38,174 --> 00:56:39,196
[SPEAKER_02]: It's because hunger is there.

948
00:56:39,776 --> 00:56:39,976
[SPEAKER_02]: That...

949
00:56:40,257 --> 00:56:41,498
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have an image of a hunger.

950
00:56:41,638 --> 00:56:42,119
[SPEAKER_02]: It's kind of...

951
00:56:42,159 --> 00:56:46,784
[SPEAKER_02]: Image of a hunger disappears, as in now gets replaced image of a non-hunger, you stop eating.

952
00:56:46,804 --> 00:56:47,624
[SPEAKER_02]: Not even a second.

953
00:56:48,185 --> 00:56:51,408
[SPEAKER_02]: What if this taste... Because all that became far removed now.

954
00:56:51,929 --> 00:56:54,171
[SPEAKER_02]: There was never the purpose of the taste to begin with.

955
00:56:54,672 --> 00:56:59,097
[SPEAKER_02]: The only reason you were even attracted to tastes is because of the pressure of the hunger.

956
00:57:00,873 --> 00:57:08,838
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, you completely then, as a result of it, gratuitously assume taste to being the matter of things that you can somehow access through your eating.

957
00:57:09,719 --> 00:57:11,680
[SPEAKER_02]: Access the external world.

958
00:57:11,760 --> 00:57:12,941
[SPEAKER_02]: That's another image in your mind.

959
00:57:13,801 --> 00:57:16,283
[SPEAKER_02]: But you think, that's not... that's not an image.

960
00:57:16,363 --> 00:57:16,943
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the world.

961
00:57:16,983 --> 00:57:17,724
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the reality.

962
00:57:19,025 --> 00:57:22,467
[SPEAKER_02]: And by doing so, you always remain bound to then that extent of assumption.

963
00:57:23,748 --> 00:57:27,250
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the sensuality, the central thirst, wanting the object.

964
00:57:28,207 --> 00:57:32,090
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you want the object unless there is an image of that you're wanting in your mind?

965
00:57:33,871 --> 00:57:35,592
[SPEAKER_02]: And then we... what is the object?

966
00:57:35,652 --> 00:57:41,857
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if I'm consuming, crushing, eating, devouring, enjoying, whatever, object...

967
00:57:41,877 --> 00:57:42,938
[SPEAKER_02]: It's another image.

968
00:57:43,078 --> 00:57:43,778
[SPEAKER_02]: It's another image.

969
00:57:45,700 --> 00:57:46,260
[SPEAKER_02]: Inescapable.

970
00:57:46,400 --> 00:57:47,461
[SPEAKER_02]: You're fully within that.

971
00:57:48,542 --> 00:57:57,148
[SPEAKER_02]: And when you fully understand it, you cannot even conceive outside of it, you stop conceiving outside of it, which is central domain, which is the assumption of centrality.

972
00:58:02,912 --> 00:58:04,914
[SPEAKER_00]: So contemplating images...

973
00:58:06,235 --> 00:58:10,117
[SPEAKER_02]: Contemplating images, not in an abstract sense, but in a sense that they're already there.

974
00:58:10,237 --> 00:58:11,078
[SPEAKER_02]: Discerning the images.

975
00:58:11,278 --> 00:58:13,019
[SPEAKER_02]: Contemplating the images means discerning the images.

976
00:58:13,300 --> 00:58:16,922
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever it is, my experience as a whole, my body, image of the body present here.

977
00:58:18,263 --> 00:58:19,044
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't need to look at it.

978
00:58:20,245 --> 00:58:24,108
[SPEAKER_02]: The fact that I can say my body is here, even if I close my eyes, means I have an image of it.

979
00:58:24,868 --> 00:58:31,213
[SPEAKER_02]: The fact that then I can know what to look at, when I say my body is here, means I have an image that's there.

980
00:58:32,700 --> 00:58:39,061
[SPEAKER_02]: How would you even know where to look for it, unless the image already implies the direction of it, the space, the space of this position, and so on?

981
00:58:42,262 --> 00:58:47,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I mean, I mean, I've got my eyes closed, I've got my eyes open, still an image, like, there's nothing...

982
00:58:47,683 --> 00:58:52,385
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just, yeah, there is an image of the senses and the image of the mind.

983
00:58:53,225 --> 00:58:54,005
[SPEAKER_02]: But the image is there.

984
00:58:57,766 --> 00:58:58,486
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can have both.

985
00:58:59,518 --> 00:59:02,479
[SPEAKER_02]: The image is there, while the image of the senses and the image of the mind is there.

986
00:59:03,059 --> 00:59:05,100
[SPEAKER_02]: But you can't attend to it to the same extent.

987
00:59:05,821 --> 00:59:06,421
[SPEAKER_02]: It has to be one.

988
00:59:06,441 --> 00:59:08,502
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where the six senses are.

989
00:59:08,522 --> 00:59:15,504
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the image is there, and the six types of images are there, on account of the six senses being there.

990
00:59:19,906 --> 00:59:23,087
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the sense bases.

991
00:59:23,167 --> 00:59:24,508
[SPEAKER_02]: saḷāyatana, ayatana,

992
00:59:32,303 --> 00:59:34,625
[SPEAKER_02]: That's already an image within which, then, the sights come.

993
00:59:35,946 --> 00:59:37,747
[SPEAKER_02]: Which are further images within that extent.

994
00:59:38,568 --> 00:59:42,971
[SPEAKER_02]: If there is no field where sights are, sights will not be manifested at all.

995
00:59:46,373 --> 00:59:49,276
[SPEAKER_02]: So, understanding the six sense bases means you overcome sensuality.

996
00:59:50,296 --> 00:59:56,241
[SPEAKER_02]: Not by dealing, managing, getting rid of the objects of your sense bases.

997
00:59:56,661 --> 00:59:56,821
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

998
00:59:57,262 --> 00:59:58,863
[SPEAKER_02]: Uprooting the framework of the bases.

999
00:59:59,263 --> 01:00:01,805
[SPEAKER_02]: Uprooting is removing ownership, misconceiving,

1000
01:00:03,806 --> 01:00:20,855
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why, like, suttas that deal with this, that talk about this, often, like, for people, they're just abstract, sounds abstract, sounds... And then, the idea of abstraction from a mind that cannot understand the nature of abstraction,

1001
01:00:21,696 --> 01:00:28,361
[SPEAKER_02]: Then you end up assuming the extension of this already gratuitously assumed external material reality.

1002
01:00:28,382 --> 01:00:34,507
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you think all these meditation experiences get to pertain to the extension of this weirdly assumed external reality.

1003
01:00:37,589 --> 01:00:38,410
[SPEAKER_02]: Like infinite space.

1004
01:00:40,011 --> 01:00:43,674
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the infinity of space.

1005
01:00:43,694 --> 01:00:45,115
[SPEAKER_02]: Neither perception nor perception.

1006
01:00:45,375 --> 01:00:50,920
[SPEAKER_02]: It becomes immediately like this transcendental universe elsewhere.

1007
01:00:59,513 --> 01:01:20,365
[SPEAKER_02]: Whereas, in fact, there are images that actually are present, but need to be discerned in order to, sort of, transcend... Yeah, that's why Sariputra said that in Majjhima, one by one, when he's developing all the jhānas and then all the base of infinite space and so on, he would ask himself, oh, is there more than this?

1008
01:01:20,765 --> 01:01:21,586
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, is there higher?

1009
01:01:21,846 --> 01:01:22,746
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there greater than this?

1010
01:01:23,707 --> 01:01:25,308
[SPEAKER_02]: But he wouldn't go around to find it.

1011
01:01:25,748 --> 01:01:28,490
[SPEAKER_02]: He would just look at the same thing and then see that there is more to it.

1012
01:01:30,037 --> 01:01:39,082
[SPEAKER_02]: Let me find another piece of log that's more liquid than the solid.

1013
01:01:39,122 --> 01:01:39,262
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

1014
01:01:39,542 --> 01:01:40,643
[SPEAKER_02]: It's within the same.

1015
01:01:40,683 --> 01:01:41,383
[SPEAKER_02]: There is more.

1016
01:01:42,223 --> 01:01:43,824
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's more on the level of the images.

1017
01:01:44,264 --> 01:01:45,345
[SPEAKER_02]: Not more on the level of...

1018
01:01:47,373 --> 01:01:52,154
[SPEAKER_02]: Fantasizing about theory of linear connections between things that you already assume to be.

1019
01:01:52,274 --> 01:02:00,336
[SPEAKER_01]: And, in fact, isn't each progressive stage more like the sort of negative of what's here?

1020
01:02:00,376 --> 01:02:00,836
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, yeah.

1021
01:02:01,296 --> 01:02:04,717
[SPEAKER_01]: But that negative is also an apartheid thing.

1022
01:02:04,857 --> 01:02:09,198
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why there is, even in Putujan experience, there is the element of nibbāna present.

1023
01:02:09,258 --> 01:02:09,798
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

1024
01:02:10,618 --> 01:02:14,299
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why some people, some traditions, completely then

1025
01:02:14,835 --> 01:02:17,455
[SPEAKER_02]: Jump to conclusion saying, ah, so we are already in nibbāna.

1026
01:02:18,436 --> 01:02:20,336
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the Buddha said, we all have element of nibbāna.

1027
01:02:20,356 --> 01:02:20,616
[SPEAKER_02]: We do.

1028
01:02:21,276 --> 01:02:23,336
[SPEAKER_02]: Element means present absence.

1029
01:02:25,397 --> 01:02:26,897
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's present as an absence.

1030
01:02:28,197 --> 01:02:31,518
[SPEAKER_02]: Means it's possible to discern it.

1031
01:02:31,778 --> 01:02:32,478
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't create it.

1032
01:02:32,498 --> 01:02:33,538
[SPEAKER_02]: Bring the context of it.

1033
01:02:33,558 --> 01:02:35,559
[SPEAKER_02]: Develop the theme of it, as the Buddha would say.

1034
01:02:35,779 --> 01:02:37,239
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you develop it.

1035
01:02:39,039 --> 01:02:43,000
[SPEAKER_02]: But saying that present absence means I'm already in that which is absent.

1036
01:02:43,652 --> 01:02:47,374
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's present as an absence, well, that's jumping the gun.

1037
01:02:49,535 --> 01:02:51,877
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there is the element of nibbāna in everyone's experience.

1038
01:02:52,157 --> 01:02:56,880
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the actual nibbāna, there is the presence of it in everyone's experience.

1039
01:02:58,601 --> 01:03:04,364
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's another good thing to reflect, because you stop assuming it to be at the end of the world, or at the end of your meditation, observational technique.

1040
01:03:05,065 --> 01:03:05,825
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it's already here.

1041
01:03:06,185 --> 01:03:08,967
[SPEAKER_02]: Every minute of your life, element of nibbāna is with you.

1042
01:03:10,641 --> 01:03:13,562
[SPEAKER_02]: Understand the theme of that element.

1043
01:03:14,983 --> 01:03:18,904
[SPEAKER_02]: Understand the characteristics of that absence.

1044
01:03:19,745 --> 01:03:22,486
[SPEAKER_02]: That is nibbāna for you.

1045
01:03:22,946 --> 01:03:25,267
[SPEAKER_02]: Absence of greed, absence of aversion, absence of delusion.

1046
01:03:25,427 --> 01:03:27,288
[SPEAKER_01]: And it shares the qualities of all things.

1047
01:03:27,328 --> 01:03:28,709
[SPEAKER_01]: We've been talking about this kind of...

1048
01:03:34,355 --> 01:03:37,841
[SPEAKER_02]: So, is there beyond, when Sariputta asks, oh, is there more than this?

1049
01:03:37,861 --> 01:03:39,002
[SPEAKER_02]: It means exactly that.

1050
01:03:39,503 --> 01:03:42,127
[SPEAKER_02]: Discerning it as a present absence.

1051
01:03:42,308 --> 01:03:45,232
[SPEAKER_02]: Discerning it as a possibility to develop and dwell on.

1052
01:03:45,673 --> 01:03:49,259
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, later on, as the sūtra says, he will develop it.

1053
01:03:50,257 --> 01:03:50,517
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah.

1054
01:03:51,057 --> 01:03:53,017
[SPEAKER_02]: He verified that there is more, there was more.

1055
01:03:53,418 --> 01:03:58,698
[SPEAKER_02]: Now that present absent is not absent anymore, because he fully discerned the context of that which was absent.

1056
01:03:59,279 --> 01:04:03,839
[SPEAKER_02]: If that was not even present, even a tiny bit, as an absence, you couldn't go in that direction.

1057
01:04:03,959 --> 01:04:05,900
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you discern something that doesn't exist?

1058
01:04:07,020 --> 01:04:08,140
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence the element of nibbāna.

1059
01:04:08,160 --> 01:04:09,940
[SPEAKER_02]: You know the all the dātras the Buddha lists.

1060
01:04:10,460 --> 01:04:15,621
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not the physical elements scattered in the world that you collect like gems or something.

1061
01:04:16,702 --> 01:04:18,902
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it's the basically the present absences.

1062
01:04:19,887 --> 01:04:23,809
[SPEAKER_02]: The absences of all, the absence of dispassion, all these themes are present absences.

1063
01:04:23,869 --> 01:04:29,991
[SPEAKER_02]: All possibilities to develop in your practice are elements, as in they're present as absences.

1064
01:04:32,212 --> 01:04:34,393
[SPEAKER_02]: But again, it has to be an image to that extent.

1065
01:04:35,424 --> 01:04:40,605
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you're a complete, utter, sensual puthujjana, nibbāna, nibbāna, what is nibbāna?

1066
01:04:40,685 --> 01:04:43,006
[SPEAKER_02]: You have some image, even if it's an image of a word.

1067
01:04:43,606 --> 01:04:44,266
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what I mean.

1068
01:04:44,746 --> 01:04:53,809
[SPEAKER_02]: It closer than represents, that ambiguous image, closer represents what nibbāna is in your experience, than when you get to, like, theorize what nibbāna is.

1069
01:04:53,829 --> 01:04:59,230
[SPEAKER_02]: Because at least you're looking in the direction of that absence, that is present as such.

1070
01:05:01,686 --> 01:05:05,168
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why some monks needed very little detailed instruction.

1071
01:05:05,348 --> 01:05:06,269
[SPEAKER_02]: Some monks needed more.

1072
01:05:08,390 --> 01:05:19,057
[SPEAKER_02]: With a minimum instruction, they would have turned to the right direction in the right absence, and then dwell on it, and understand the context of it, and by doing so, develop it.

1073
01:05:20,378 --> 01:05:21,719
[SPEAKER_02]: Even jhāna is the same principle.

1074
01:05:22,439 --> 01:05:24,461
[SPEAKER_02]: The Buddha would say, develop the theme of jhāna.

1075
01:05:25,461 --> 01:05:29,184
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, jhānas are basically so close to the point of view, that's why people

1076
01:05:30,295 --> 01:05:32,937
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it takes effort to attain it, to attain it, to develop it.

1077
01:05:34,818 --> 01:05:37,901
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like jhāna is figuring something out at the end of a certain effort.

1078
01:05:38,861 --> 01:05:42,024
[SPEAKER_02]: The jhāna is basically the way you're seeing regarding things.

1079
01:05:43,300 --> 01:05:46,722
[SPEAKER_02]: That you then got polluted with your own ignorance, with your own lust, aversion and habits.

1080
01:05:47,042 --> 01:05:53,846
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's removal of this pollution to basically recognize that certain imperturbable basis, unified basis.

1081
01:05:54,407 --> 01:06:07,094
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, whether I think about the sense object, whether I think about joy, grief, an image has to be there enduring, and you become discerning of the enduring image regardless of the content of whatever points you.

1082
01:06:07,114 --> 01:06:08,595
[SPEAKER_02]: That's already a unified mind.

1083
01:06:09,675 --> 01:06:12,217
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's already jhāna on account of any experience you can have.

1084
01:06:14,687 --> 01:06:23,370
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why all those different... different ways of entering jhāna, with commentaries compiled from the suttas, but like looking at the corpse.

1085
01:06:23,950 --> 01:06:24,290
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1086
01:06:24,350 --> 01:06:25,030
[SPEAKER_02]: Establish jhāna.

1087
01:06:25,070 --> 01:06:25,791
[SPEAKER_02]: Mindfulness of the body.

1088
01:06:25,811 --> 01:06:26,471
[SPEAKER_02]: Establish jhāna.

1089
01:06:26,491 --> 01:06:28,111
[SPEAKER_02]: So, like, this results in jhāna.

1090
01:06:28,411 --> 01:06:33,053
[SPEAKER_02]: Jhāna is an attitude of unification upon certain discernments.

1091
01:06:33,913 --> 01:06:36,054
[SPEAKER_02]: Discernment of dispassion, discernment of... Yeah.

1092
01:06:36,174 --> 01:06:38,515
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, like, sensuality, abandoning it, and so on.

1093
01:06:38,575 --> 01:06:39,095
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not...

1094
01:06:40,333 --> 01:06:41,894
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not the content, it's the...

1095
01:06:41,914 --> 01:06:42,695
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the thing.

1096
01:06:42,795 --> 01:06:43,175
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

1097
01:06:43,315 --> 01:06:44,276
[SPEAKER_02]: The nature of the image.

1098
01:06:44,296 --> 01:06:44,616
[SPEAKER_02]: The thing.

1099
01:06:44,636 --> 01:06:46,057
[SPEAKER_02]: The context of... Yeah, exactly.

1100
01:06:46,217 --> 01:06:48,218
[SPEAKER_02]: The characteristics of the unified mind.

1101
01:06:58,285 --> 01:06:58,986
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's always...

1102
01:06:59,606 --> 01:07:09,553
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter the experience, whether I'm feeling good, I'm feeling bad, I'm in physical discomfort, physical comfort, whatever it is.

1103
01:07:11,705 --> 01:07:20,149
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter how complex it is, how ambiguous it is, how content kind of inclined it is, image is there, has to be there.

1104
01:07:20,989 --> 01:07:29,854
[SPEAKER_02]: Any idea of direction, any idea of the movement, to, for, against, both, neither, requires an image to be there and during.

1105
01:07:30,134 --> 01:07:31,434
[SPEAKER_02]: Some ambiguous image.

1106
01:07:32,075 --> 01:07:36,537
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's recognizing, learning how to discern an image in its ambiguity.

1107
01:07:37,384 --> 01:07:42,287
[SPEAKER_02]: Not try to replace it with content that you deemed a worthy threshold of.

1108
01:07:46,190 --> 01:07:52,895
[SPEAKER_02]: Because how can you be exercising the threshold of necessary content, unless there is an image that needs to be built up to that level?

1109
01:07:53,595 --> 01:07:54,996
[SPEAKER_02]: I just think it's about being skilled.

1110
01:07:55,016 --> 01:07:58,939
[SPEAKER_02]: Like you said, being skilled in images, which is being skilled in dhamma.

1111
01:07:59,139 --> 01:07:59,999
[SPEAKER_02]: Being skilled in the mind.

1112
01:08:01,090 --> 01:08:07,173
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing the mind quickly, you know, like, as soon as it arises, as soon as the image is manifest, he sees it exactly for what it is.

1113
01:08:07,933 --> 01:08:11,835
[SPEAKER_02]: He doesn't need to, oh, catch himself down the line, and then, what?

1114
01:08:11,855 --> 01:08:13,216
[SPEAKER_02]: You will need that in the beginning.

1115
01:08:13,536 --> 01:08:16,277
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just like, like, the image, the word for image is dhamma.

1116
01:08:16,637 --> 01:08:16,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

1117
01:08:17,378 --> 01:08:18,438
[SPEAKER_02]: Being skilled in dhamma, yeah.

1118
01:08:18,879 --> 01:08:21,220
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why this teaching is the teaching, the dhamma.

1119
01:08:21,580 --> 01:08:21,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1120
01:08:21,840 --> 01:08:22,400
[SPEAKER_02]: The image.

1121
01:08:22,600 --> 01:08:22,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1122
01:08:23,221 --> 01:08:27,783
[SPEAKER_02]: The Buddha was teaching the image, the fundamental image, the universal image,

1123
01:08:28,647 --> 01:08:35,729
[SPEAKER_02]: In common to any five aggregates, anywhere in the... any universal realm, it's the image.

1124
01:08:36,209 --> 01:08:39,589
[SPEAKER_02]: Understanding... and that image is understanding the nature of images.

1125
01:08:39,629 --> 01:08:39,929
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1126
01:08:40,069 --> 01:08:41,130
[SPEAKER_00]: The extent of images.

1127
01:08:41,190 --> 01:08:42,030
[SPEAKER_00]: The extent of images.

1128
01:08:42,530 --> 01:08:43,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Image extent.

1129
01:08:44,170 --> 01:08:46,751
[SPEAKER_02]: Not image extent in terms of telling you how many there are.

1130
01:08:47,291 --> 01:08:48,391
[SPEAKER_02]: The extent of the nature.

1131
01:08:48,731 --> 01:08:55,473
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like a little... like, if he was talking about all these images, it's because it was the context of understanding the nature.

1132
01:08:55,493 --> 01:08:56,713
[SPEAKER_02]: And what is the nature of images?

1133
01:08:58,152 --> 01:08:58,733
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be owned.

1134
01:08:59,193 --> 01:09:00,815
[SPEAKER_02]: It cannot be appropriated.

1135
01:09:01,796 --> 01:09:02,617
[SPEAKER_02]: It cannot be yours.

1136
01:09:02,777 --> 01:09:03,758
[SPEAKER_02]: It cannot be controlled.

1137
01:09:04,399 --> 01:09:06,962
[SPEAKER_02]: You are the one subjected to them, not other way around.

1138
01:09:07,402 --> 01:09:11,406
[SPEAKER_02]: You understand that, you are free from the entire saṃsara, all of it.

1139
01:09:12,027 --> 01:09:12,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely.

1140
01:09:13,269 --> 01:09:13,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing left.

1141
01:09:17,273 --> 01:09:19,095
[SPEAKER_02]: Saṃsara, universe, rebirth.

1142
01:09:21,325 --> 01:09:23,626
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, all these different directions, what do they have in common?

1143
01:09:23,767 --> 01:09:24,507
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, they are an image.

1144
01:09:25,387 --> 01:09:26,508
[SPEAKER_02]: Not this image, not that image.

1145
01:09:26,528 --> 01:09:30,911
[SPEAKER_02]: A specific, like, you know, maybe ambiguous, but as such, an image is there.

1146
01:09:31,031 --> 01:09:31,211
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1147
01:09:31,751 --> 01:09:33,452
[SPEAKER_02]: What else is there when I have these images?

1148
01:09:33,653 --> 01:09:34,533
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, it's always me.

1149
01:09:34,693 --> 01:09:37,355
[SPEAKER_02]: A sense of... another image of me is always there.

1150
01:09:37,495 --> 01:09:37,675
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1151
01:09:38,035 --> 01:09:41,137
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's kind of more in common to all these other things that you've been dwelling on.

1152
01:09:41,197 --> 01:09:46,741
[SPEAKER_02]: So, like, why don't you dwell on something that's more in common to those particular things, as opposed to chasing down particular leads?

1153
01:09:46,841 --> 01:09:47,101
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1154
01:09:47,641 --> 01:09:54,925
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how inevitably you will end up to, like, self-awareness, responsibility, point of view, all these things we talk about.

1155
01:09:54,965 --> 01:10:12,973
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to go and have refined images.

1156
01:10:14,274 --> 01:10:15,715
[SPEAKER_02]: There wouldn't be refined images.

1157
01:10:18,591 --> 01:10:21,293
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're refining your images, it means you're not seeing them as images.

1158
01:10:22,054 --> 01:10:25,677
[SPEAKER_02]: You're basically chasing the content, which is another image that you fail to see.

1159
01:10:26,197 --> 01:10:31,141
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence the idea of refinement, of replacing it with another image of a different kind.

1160
01:10:31,762 --> 01:10:33,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Ambiguous images.

1161
01:10:34,004 --> 01:10:39,148
[SPEAKER_02]: The true refinement of an ambiguous image is to recognize its ambiguity to be an image.

1162
01:10:40,729 --> 01:10:41,530
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you refine it.

1163
01:10:41,990 --> 01:10:44,793
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't refine it by providing content for it.

1164
01:10:45,818 --> 01:10:47,479
[SPEAKER_02]: That's psychologization, basically.

1165
01:10:48,199 --> 01:10:50,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, just now, we were talking this whole idea of content.

1166
01:10:50,480 --> 01:10:52,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Every time you say content, I just think that's meaningless.

1167
01:10:53,441 --> 01:10:56,963
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, once you establish your mind upon the unified image, then it's meaningless.

1168
01:10:57,483 --> 01:10:59,524
[SPEAKER_02]: Being distracted becomes inconceivable.

1169
01:10:59,984 --> 01:11:00,504
[SPEAKER_02]: Meaningless.

1170
01:11:01,525 --> 01:11:01,765
Yeah.

1171
01:11:04,186 --> 01:11:04,966
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, it ceases.

1172
01:11:05,306 --> 01:11:06,087
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not there anymore.

1173
01:11:06,107 --> 01:11:06,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1174
01:11:17,670 --> 01:11:19,611
[SPEAKER_02]: Your mood is an image.

1175
01:11:21,332 --> 01:11:23,393
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1176
01:11:23,793 --> 01:11:27,894
[SPEAKER_02]: Has a shape, has a color to it, darkness, lightness.

1177
01:11:27,914 --> 01:11:29,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes it's a name, sometimes...

1178
01:11:29,375 --> 01:11:30,596
[SPEAKER_02]: It has weight to it, yeah.

1179
01:11:30,696 --> 01:11:39,980
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why the Buddha described the satipaṭṭhāna for citta, elated, expensive, shrunk, depressed, elated, big, small mind.

1180
01:11:40,680 --> 01:11:43,621
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, yeah, those are images that mind is.

1181
01:11:44,061 --> 01:11:44,922
[SPEAKER_02]: That is what mind is.

1182
01:11:45,930 --> 01:11:47,471
[SPEAKER_02]: Not I'm thinking elation.

